1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,755
You say: Lander, what time is it?

2
00:00:03,015 --> 00:00:06,024
And in under a second,
the Lander answers you, out loud.

3
00:00:06,374 --> 00:00:09,002
But between your voice
and its voice, a lot happens.

4
00:00:09,262 --> 00:00:09,870
Checks.

5
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:11,791
Models that load and unload.

6
00:00:12,051 --> 00:00:13,129
Vectors of meaning.

7
00:00:13,389 --> 00:00:17,333
And decisions made by plain code,
and only sometimes by the AI.

8
00:00:17,683 --> 00:00:21,424
In this video we follow one order
step by step, from the wake word

9
00:00:21,424 --> 00:00:23,918
to the moment the assistant stops talking.

10
00:00:24,178 --> 00:00:25,130
Nothing skipped.

11
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:28,547
This is the map.

12
00:00:28,807 --> 00:00:29,698
The microphone.

13
00:00:29,958 --> 00:00:31,834
The wake word, and how it is checked.

14
00:00:32,094 --> 00:00:34,173
Listening to the order until you stop.

15
00:00:34,433 --> 00:00:35,641
Turning it into text.

16
00:00:35,901 --> 00:00:37,323
Handing it to the assistant.

17
00:00:37,583 --> 00:00:41,488
Deciding who deals with it: a rule,
one of your commands, or the AI.

18
00:00:41,748 --> 00:00:42,484
The tools.

19
00:00:42,744 --> 00:00:44,068
And finally, the voice.

20
00:00:44,418 --> 00:00:48,166
And one idea runs through the whole
journey: cheap and safe first.

21
00:00:48,426 --> 00:00:52,339
Whatever rules and comparisons
can settle never reaches the AI.

22
00:00:52,599 --> 00:00:54,593
The AI only gets what is left over.

23
00:00:56,993 --> 00:00:58,174
First, who does what.

24
00:00:58,434 --> 00:00:59,764
There are two processes.

25
00:01:00,114 --> 00:01:02,363
K-Desktop runs as administrator.

26
00:01:02,623 --> 00:01:05,016
It owns the microphone, the speech models,

27
00:01:05,016 --> 00:01:08,408
your commands, and the little window
that asks for permission.

28
00:01:08,758 --> 00:01:13,658
The assistants live in another process,
as a normal user, with no admin rights.

29
00:01:13,918 --> 00:01:16,647
That is where their brain is,
their tools, their voice,

30
00:01:16,647 --> 00:01:18,527
and the character you see on screen.

31
00:01:18,877 --> 00:01:22,291
They talk over a named pipe:
one JSON message per line.

32
00:01:22,551 --> 00:01:26,582
K-Desktop lets only your user in,
and checks that whoever connects

33
00:01:26,582 --> 00:01:31,285
is the same program, in your session,
and the very process it launched itself.

34
00:01:31,635 --> 00:01:34,511
What an assistant may ask
for is a closed list:

35
00:01:34,511 --> 00:01:38,636
run commands that already exist,
move windows, get vectors of meaning.

36
00:01:38,896 --> 00:01:42,317
Never an arbitrary program
or script with admin rights.

37
00:01:42,667 --> 00:01:46,373
After five seconds with no traffic,
they send each other a heartbeat.

38
00:01:46,633 --> 00:01:50,191
If the assistant process crashes,
K-Desktop starts it again,

39
00:01:50,191 --> 00:01:52,348
waiting a little longer each time.

40
00:01:54,598 --> 00:01:55,614
The journey starts.

41
00:01:55,874 --> 00:01:59,370
The microphone is always open,
but nothing leaves your PC.

42
00:01:59,630 --> 00:02:04,265
First comes echo cancellation: whatever
your speakers are playing is subtracted,

43
00:02:04,265 --> 00:02:06,528
including the assistant's own voice.

44
00:02:06,878 --> 00:02:11,734
Every 80 milliseconds, openWakeWord
turns the sound into a spectrogram,

45
00:02:11,734 --> 00:02:16,590
and then into an embedding: 96 numbers
that sum up that slice of audio.

46
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:20,895
Your personal detector looks at the last
16 of them, just over a second,

47
00:02:20,895 --> 00:02:22,365
and gives two opinions.

48
00:02:22,625 --> 00:02:25,799
One: how close this is
to your own recordings of the word.

49
00:02:26,059 --> 00:02:29,578
The other: a classifier,
trained on your PC with your takes,

50
00:02:29,578 --> 00:02:32,403
against silence, noise,
and your normal speech.

51
00:02:32,753 --> 00:02:35,737
The final score is
the geometric mean of the two.

52
00:02:35,997 --> 00:02:38,107
If either one says no, the score sinks.

53
00:02:38,367 --> 00:02:39,504
Both have to agree.

54
00:02:39,854 --> 00:02:43,063
If it passes the threshold,
still nothing visible happens.

55
00:02:43,323 --> 00:02:46,383
No sound, no notice,
the music is not turned down.

56
00:02:46,643 --> 00:02:50,096
First, phase two: Parakeet,
the speech recognition model,

57
00:02:50,096 --> 00:02:54,864
transcribes just the clip of the word,
and that text has to sound like your word.

58
00:02:55,214 --> 00:02:59,098
If the detector was very sure,
a slightly worse transcription is enough.

59
00:02:59,358 --> 00:03:02,192
And if even that fails,
the word stays in doubt,

60
00:03:02,192 --> 00:03:06,339
and is confirmed later, by transcribing
the word together with the order.

61
00:03:06,689 --> 00:03:07,705
Two more filters.

62
00:03:07,965 --> 00:03:11,496
If the transcription matches
something an assistant has just said,

63
00:03:11,496 --> 00:03:13,898
it is its own voice,
and it is thrown away.

64
00:03:14,158 --> 00:03:16,597
And if the word only comes up in passing,

65
00:03:16,597 --> 00:03:21,059
as in: I was showing my brother the Lander,
you are talking about it, not to it.

66
00:03:21,409 --> 00:03:23,153
Past all that: whose word is it?

67
00:03:23,413 --> 00:03:25,487
Every recorded word has one owner.

68
00:03:25,747 --> 00:03:29,600
K-Desktop's commands,
one particular assistant, or nobody.

69
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:31,841
The word Lander belongs to the Lander.

70
00:03:32,191 --> 00:03:33,135
Now it happens.

71
00:03:33,395 --> 00:03:35,255
Other sounds are turned down,

72
00:03:35,255 --> 00:03:39,720
the listening sound plays, and K-Desktop
sends the Lander a listening message.

73
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:44,404
The character looks like it is listening,
its own voice drops to twenty percent,

74
00:03:44,404 --> 00:03:47,593
and if its model is already
in memory, it is kept there.

75
00:03:47,943 --> 00:03:50,278
There are other ways in,
with no wake word.

76
00:03:50,538 --> 00:03:51,884
Hold the assistant's key.

77
00:03:52,144 --> 00:03:54,202
Hold a click on it
for about half a second.

78
00:03:54,462 --> 00:03:55,198
Type to it.

79
00:03:55,458 --> 00:03:56,151
Your phone.

80
00:03:56,411 --> 00:03:57,110
A command.

81
00:03:57,370 --> 00:03:58,959
Or an order scheduled for later.

82
00:03:59,219 --> 00:04:00,832
They all end up in the same place.

83
00:04:03,082 --> 00:04:06,400
While the word was being checked,
the audio never stopped recording.

84
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:10,187
So the order is not lost,
even if you say it all in one breath.

85
00:04:10,537 --> 00:04:12,570
Silero, a voice activity detector,

86
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:15,620
tells the stretches
where you speak from the silences.

87
00:04:15,970 --> 00:04:17,388
At every short pause,

88
00:04:17,388 --> 00:04:21,539
a quarter of a second, Parakeet already
transcribes what was said so far.

89
00:04:21,799 --> 00:04:26,519
If the order ends right there, that text
is used, and nothing is transcribed twice.

90
00:04:26,869 --> 00:04:30,208
The order ends after 800
milliseconds of silence.

91
00:04:30,468 --> 00:04:32,695
That is the floor of the response time:

92
00:04:32,695 --> 00:04:35,863
any shorter, and orders
with a pause in them would be cut.

93
00:04:36,123 --> 00:04:39,106
If you say nothing for five seconds,
listening stops.

94
00:04:39,366 --> 00:04:40,907
And fifteen seconds at most.

95
00:04:41,257 --> 00:04:42,502
Then the text is chosen.

96
00:04:42,762 --> 00:04:44,232
There are several candidates.

97
00:04:44,492 --> 00:04:45,900
What was said after a pause.

98
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:49,613
The word and the order transcribed
together, with the word cut out.

99
00:04:49,873 --> 00:04:52,835
Or the order alone,
when the other version lost words.

100
00:04:53,185 --> 00:04:57,964
The echo guard again: if the text is what
an assistant was saying, it is ignored.

101
00:04:58,314 --> 00:05:02,918
And finally, K-Desktop sends the assistant
process a heard message: the text,

102
00:05:02,918 --> 00:05:05,359
who it is for, and where it came from.

103
00:05:05,619 --> 00:05:06,940
By voice, in this case.

104
00:05:09,190 --> 00:05:10,772
The order reaches the assistant.

105
00:05:11,032 --> 00:05:13,543
Each assistant has its own conversation,

106
00:05:13,543 --> 00:05:16,326
and a new order replaces
the one still going.

107
00:05:16,586 --> 00:05:19,956
Tell it to stop while it talks,
and what it was doing is cancelled.

108
00:05:20,306 --> 00:05:23,013
What you said appears in the chat,
and goes into the log.

109
00:05:23,363 --> 00:05:27,008
If the order comes with files,
because you dropped them on the character,

110
00:05:27,008 --> 00:05:28,531
it goes straight to the AI.

111
00:05:28,791 --> 00:05:30,423
Otherwise, the routing begins.

112
00:05:30,773 --> 00:05:33,688
And here is the key point:
who decides what gets done?

113
00:05:33,948 --> 00:05:34,818
Not the AI.

114
00:05:35,078 --> 00:05:38,062
Code decides, in layers,
always in the same order.

115
00:05:38,322 --> 00:05:39,663
First, the fixed rules.

116
00:05:39,923 --> 00:05:40,952
Then your commands.

117
00:05:41,212 --> 00:05:42,185
Then programs.

118
00:05:42,445 --> 00:05:44,560
And only what is left reaches the AI.

119
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:49,940
Fixed rules are patterns written
in the language files,

120
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:51,650
for every installed language.

121
00:05:51,910 --> 00:05:53,683
They have gaps: a duration,

122
00:05:53,683 --> 00:05:57,339
like ten minutes, or free text,
like what you want noted down.

123
00:05:57,599 --> 00:05:58,784
And optional words.

124
00:05:59,134 --> 00:06:02,036
Before comparing,
the assistant's name is removed,

125
00:06:02,036 --> 00:06:05,974
so is filler, like hey or please,
and polite openings, like could you.

126
00:06:06,234 --> 00:06:08,183
The whole order has to fit a pattern.

127
00:06:08,533 --> 00:06:10,880
That is how these are answered at once:

128
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:14,739
the time, a timer, a note, cancel,
repeat that, undo, remember that,

129
00:06:14,739 --> 00:06:18,441
the weather, write what I say,
free your brain, or think it over.

130
00:06:18,791 --> 00:06:20,777
A rule does not do things on its own.

131
00:06:21,037 --> 00:06:24,591
It calls the same tool the AI would use,
through the tool runner.

132
00:06:24,851 --> 00:06:27,789
So it shows up in the activity,
and it can be undone.

133
00:06:28,049 --> 00:06:30,274
And it answers
in the character's own words.

134
00:06:30,624 --> 00:06:34,019
One special case: at seven,
empty the recycle bin.

135
00:06:34,279 --> 00:06:35,667
Doing that now would be wrong.

136
00:06:35,927 --> 00:06:39,829
So an order for later goes to the AI,
with the schedule tool at hand.

137
00:06:40,179 --> 00:06:43,824
And if you say the exact phrase
of one of your JSON tools,

138
00:06:43,824 --> 00:06:47,183
like good night, that tool runs,
also without the AI.

139
00:06:47,533 --> 00:06:48,542
One last check.

140
00:06:48,802 --> 00:06:52,316
If you say you got that wrong,
right after an answer from the AI,

141
00:06:52,316 --> 00:06:53,950
no commands are looked for.

142
00:06:54,210 --> 00:06:55,768
The AI thinks it over again.

143
00:06:58,018 --> 00:07:02,795
If no rule fits, the assistant asks
K-Desktop: which commands look like this?

144
00:07:03,055 --> 00:07:04,375
That is the match message.

145
00:07:04,635 --> 00:07:09,067
K-Desktop searches just like its own
voice control does, but runs nothing.

146
00:07:09,327 --> 00:07:12,129
Questions,
like what does my game mode command do,

147
00:07:12,129 --> 00:07:14,131
skip this step: they go to the AI.

148
00:07:14,481 --> 00:07:15,920
The first layer is words.

149
00:07:16,180 --> 00:07:18,581
How much of a command's name is
in what you said?

150
00:07:18,841 --> 00:07:22,014
Word endings don't matter:
open, opens, opening.

151
00:07:22,274 --> 00:07:24,645
Small transcription slips are forgiven,

152
00:07:24,645 --> 00:07:28,386
words split apart or run together,
and words that sound the same.

153
00:07:28,646 --> 00:07:29,762
Filler doesn't count.

154
00:07:30,112 --> 00:07:34,264
To win on words,
a command needs a score of 0.82,

155
00:07:34,264 --> 00:07:37,510
and a lead of 0.08 over the next one.

156
00:07:37,860 --> 00:07:40,348
If the whole order names nothing clearly,

157
00:07:40,348 --> 00:07:45,005
it is split at and, then, after, and commas,
and each piece is matched on its own.

158
00:07:45,265 --> 00:07:48,895
Open YouTube
and empty the recycle bin is two commands.

159
00:07:49,155 --> 00:07:52,954
And a piece like and close it refers
to the thing in the piece before.

160
00:07:55,204 --> 00:07:58,433
When words are not enough,
the second layer kicks in: meaning.

161
00:07:58,693 --> 00:08:00,530
And this is where embeddings come in.

162
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:04,801
An embedding is a list of numbers
that stands for what a sentence means.

163
00:08:05,061 --> 00:08:09,846
EmbeddingGemma, a small model
from Google that runs on your PC,

164
00:08:09,846 --> 00:08:12,829
turns each sentence into 768 numbers.

165
00:08:13,179 --> 00:08:14,971
Think of them as a point in space.

166
00:08:15,231 --> 00:08:18,211
Sentences that mean the same
thing land close together,

167
00:08:18,211 --> 00:08:20,213
even if they share no words at all.

168
00:08:20,473 --> 00:08:23,840
Clear out the trash lands right next
to Empty Recycle Bin.

169
00:08:24,190 --> 00:08:28,224
Closeness is measured with the cosine
of the angle between the two vectors.

170
00:08:28,484 --> 00:08:29,716
One means identical.

171
00:08:29,976 --> 00:08:31,435
Zero means unrelated.

172
00:08:31,785 --> 00:08:33,223
What is your order compared with?

173
00:08:33,483 --> 00:08:37,849
Each command's name, its voice phrases,
and what its first actions say they do,

174
00:08:37,849 --> 00:08:39,595
like shut down the computer.

175
00:08:39,855 --> 00:08:44,480
Those vectors are worked out once and kept
on disk: only new names cost anything.

176
00:08:44,830 --> 00:08:49,144
A command wins on meaning from 0.62,
if it is clearly ahead.

177
00:08:49,404 --> 00:08:52,074
The built-in music orders need 0.80.

178
00:08:52,334 --> 00:08:56,766
And anything that shuts down,
restarts or signs out needs 0.90.

179
00:08:57,116 --> 00:09:01,632
It is also compared with a list of things
that are not K-Desktop's job:

180
00:09:01,632 --> 00:09:03,567
the TV, the lights, alarms.

181
00:09:03,827 --> 00:09:06,635
Turn off the TV is close
to Shut down the computer,

182
00:09:06,635 --> 00:09:08,409
but closer still to that list.

183
00:09:08,669 --> 00:09:09,685
So nothing runs.

184
00:09:10,035 --> 00:09:13,079
A detail:
the meaning model is loaded only once.

185
00:09:13,339 --> 00:09:16,701
The assistants ask K-Desktop
for their vectors over the pipe,

186
00:09:16,701 --> 00:09:18,105
with an embed message.

187
00:09:18,455 --> 00:09:20,404
K-Desktop answers with matched:

188
00:09:20,404 --> 00:09:25,037
each piece of the order, its candidates
with their scores, and whether it is sure.

189
00:09:25,297 --> 00:09:26,776
Now the assistant decides.

190
00:09:27,036 --> 00:09:31,413
Sure, and scoring at least 0.80:
it runs right away, no AI.

191
00:09:31,673 --> 00:09:37,162
Two candidates almost tied: it asks,
Empty Recycle Bin or Open Recycle Bin?

192
00:09:37,422 --> 00:09:38,793
And you answer the first one.

193
00:09:39,053 --> 00:09:39,985
Still no AI.

194
00:09:42,235 --> 00:09:43,507
And if nothing is clear?

195
00:09:43,767 --> 00:09:46,128
Before handing the whole order to the AI,

196
00:09:46,128 --> 00:09:48,991
there is a shortcut:
deciding with a single letter.

197
00:09:49,341 --> 00:09:53,101
It takes the commands closest
in meaning, up to ten,

198
00:09:53,101 --> 00:09:58,417
as long as the best one is above 0.55,
and the order has ten words or fewer.

199
00:09:58,767 --> 00:10:01,457
The assistant's own model gets a list: A,

200
00:10:01,457 --> 00:10:04,890
B, C, one letter per command,
and Z for none of these.

201
00:10:05,150 --> 00:10:07,455
And it is told to answer
with one letter only.

202
00:10:07,805 --> 00:10:09,151
Its answer is not read.

203
00:10:09,411 --> 00:10:11,875
What is read are
the probabilities of each letter.

204
00:10:12,135 --> 00:10:15,118
If one is above 0.90, that command runs.

205
00:10:15,378 --> 00:10:21,637
If two together reach 0.90, and the second
has at least 0.25, it asks which one.

206
00:10:21,897 --> 00:10:25,603
If Z wins, or nothing stands out,
the order goes to the AI.

207
00:10:25,953 --> 00:10:29,936
It takes about a tenth of a second,
instead of a whole answer from the AI.

208
00:10:30,196 --> 00:10:33,577
Measured with 117 real commands,

209
00:10:33,577 --> 00:10:39,272
it got 123 of 184 orders right
without the AI, up from 98.

210
00:10:39,532 --> 00:10:41,597
And it never shuts down the PC from here.

211
00:10:41,947 --> 00:10:46,744
If there are still pieces nobody took,
programs get a look: minimize Chrome,

212
00:10:46,744 --> 00:10:48,557
quit Spotify, open Steam.

213
00:10:48,817 --> 00:10:50,284
These are rules too,

214
00:10:50,284 --> 00:10:55,192
and K-Desktop finds the program among
the open windows and the running processes.

215
00:10:57,442 --> 00:11:00,561
When a command has to run,
the assistant sends run,

216
00:11:00,561 --> 00:11:04,665
with the command's id and choreography,
because it is on the desktop.

217
00:11:05,015 --> 00:11:08,707
K-Desktop works out its risk:
that of its riskiest action.

218
00:11:08,967 --> 00:11:10,159
Green, no question.

219
00:11:10,419 --> 00:11:12,067
Yellow, asks the first time.

220
00:11:12,327 --> 00:11:13,781
Red, asks every time.

221
00:11:14,041 --> 00:11:16,490
A command always asks
at least the first time.

222
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:19,840
The permission window appears
without stealing your focus.

223
00:11:20,100 --> 00:11:22,943
You can say yes, no,
or yes, and don't ask again.

224
00:11:23,203 --> 00:11:26,314
If the order was spoken,
the window listens by itself.

225
00:11:26,574 --> 00:11:28,614
No answer in ten seconds means no.

226
00:11:28,964 --> 00:11:30,958
Then each action arrives as a step:

227
00:11:30,958 --> 00:11:33,730
what it is, which window,
and where it will end up.

228
00:11:33,990 --> 00:11:38,238
The assistant does its gesture:
it flies to the button, aims, and fires.

229
00:11:38,498 --> 00:11:43,190
At the moment of impact it sends go,
and K-Desktop, which has the rights, does it.

230
00:11:43,540 --> 00:11:46,147
If the assistant does not answer in time,

231
00:11:46,147 --> 00:11:50,584
six seconds, or ten when it has
to tow a window, K-Desktop does it anyway.

232
00:11:50,844 --> 00:11:51,721
It never hangs.

233
00:11:51,981 --> 00:11:55,474
And if the assistant says it
did it itself, K-Desktop checks,

234
00:11:55,474 --> 00:11:57,142
and sets the exact result.

235
00:11:57,492 --> 00:12:00,882
When it is over, end arrives,
with the outcome and the messages.

236
00:12:01,142 --> 00:12:05,330
The run goes into K-Desktop's history,
with the assistant as its source.

237
00:12:05,680 --> 00:12:07,437
Minimize Chrome works the same way.

238
00:12:07,697 --> 00:12:09,091
With an act message,

239
00:12:09,091 --> 00:12:13,110
K-Desktop builds a throwaway command
from the actions in its catalog.

240
00:12:13,370 --> 00:12:17,556
It asks like a command, appears
in the history, and gets the same gestures.

241
00:12:19,806 --> 00:12:20,658
Now, the AI.

242
00:12:20,918 --> 00:12:24,088
It only gets what the rules
and the commands left undone.

243
00:12:24,438 --> 00:12:25,881
First: is there a model?

244
00:12:26,141 --> 00:12:29,921
The brain runs on llama server
on your PC, on Ollama,

245
00:12:29,921 --> 00:12:32,548
or on any OpenAI compatible server.

246
00:12:32,808 --> 00:12:34,615
If the model is not loaded yet,

247
00:12:34,615 --> 00:12:38,328
the assistant says one moment straight
away, so you know it heard you.

248
00:12:38,678 --> 00:12:42,803
If two and a half seconds go by with nothing
said, it says a waiting phrase.

249
00:12:43,063 --> 00:12:45,767
But not again for three minutes:
that would get tiring.

250
00:12:46,117 --> 00:12:48,021
Then the conversation is put together.

251
00:12:48,281 --> 00:12:52,125
The instructions: who it is,
its personality, how to use tools,

252
00:12:52,125 --> 00:12:56,359
short sentences a voice can say,
your language, and the date and time.

253
00:12:56,709 --> 00:13:00,021
What it remembers about you,
when it fits: found by meaning,

254
00:13:00,021 --> 00:13:02,178
comparing the order with each memory.

255
00:13:02,438 --> 00:13:04,771
Your commands that K-Desktop saw close by.

256
00:13:05,031 --> 00:13:08,840
The last three exchanges,
forgotten after ten minutes of silence.

257
00:13:09,100 --> 00:13:10,268
And the order itself.

258
00:13:12,518 --> 00:13:17,318
There are about seventy built-in tools,
and with MCP servers there can be hundreds.

259
00:13:17,578 --> 00:13:21,047
But a small model chooses well
among a few, and badly among many.

260
00:13:21,307 --> 00:13:23,179
And every tool costs instructions.

261
00:13:23,529 --> 00:13:26,964
So every tool comes with example phrases,
in your language.

262
00:13:27,224 --> 00:13:30,146
They become vectors once,
and K-Desktop keeps them.

263
00:13:30,496 --> 00:13:34,863
For each order, its vector is worked out
and compared with all those examples.

264
00:13:35,123 --> 00:13:37,804
Each tool keeps the score
of its closest example.

265
00:13:38,154 --> 00:13:41,525
At least the three best get through,
and up to five,

266
00:13:41,525 --> 00:13:44,140
if they are within 0.08 of the top one.

267
00:13:44,490 --> 00:13:48,751
If the order has several parts,
like check the weather and send it

268
00:13:48,751 --> 00:13:54,017
to me on Telegram, the best tool of each
part gets in too, if it scores above 0.60.

269
00:13:54,367 --> 00:13:56,153
And some tools are always at hand.

270
00:13:56,413 --> 00:14:00,757
The ones used in the last exchange,
so it understands and how long is left?

271
00:14:01,017 --> 00:14:03,218
The screen tools, if you say that window.

272
00:14:03,478 --> 00:14:05,540
The file tools, if you gave it a file.

273
00:14:05,800 --> 00:14:09,618
And run command, if K-Desktop
saw commands of yours close by.

274
00:14:09,968 --> 00:14:13,624
If it still lacks the one it needs,
it can look for more: find tools.

275
00:14:13,884 --> 00:14:16,690
And if the order deserves it,
it can ask to think harder.

276
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:20,126
Without the meaning model,
tools are chosen by words:

277
00:14:20,126 --> 00:14:22,637
rare words, used by few tools, weigh more.

278
00:14:22,897 --> 00:14:24,022
Worse, but it works.

279
00:14:26,272 --> 00:14:27,631
What kinds of tools are there?

280
00:14:27,891 --> 00:14:32,059
The built-in ones: timers, notes,
memory, sums and dates, the weather,

281
00:14:32,059 --> 00:14:36,612
searching and reading the web, files,
looking at the screen, the PC's status,

282
00:14:36,612 --> 00:14:38,641
the network, windows and music.

283
00:14:38,991 --> 00:14:44,130
The ones that talk to K-Desktop: running
your commands, finding and explaining them,

284
00:14:44,130 --> 00:14:47,784
firing events, handling programs,
the volume, the display,

285
00:14:47,784 --> 00:14:52,580
shutting down or locking, creating events,
scheduling orders, reading the log.

286
00:14:52,840 --> 00:14:55,689
The assistant only asks:
K-Desktop does it.

287
00:14:56,039 --> 00:14:58,830
Your own, in JSON,
with nothing to compile.

288
00:14:59,090 --> 00:14:59,928
Four types.

289
00:15:00,188 --> 00:15:03,791
http, to call a web API,
like Home Assistant.

290
00:15:04,051 --> 00:15:06,365
kdesktop, to run one of your commands.

291
00:15:06,625 --> 00:15:08,369
routine, a series of steps.

292
00:15:08,629 --> 00:15:10,290
And script, a PowerShell script.

293
00:15:10,640 --> 00:15:13,943
Secrets are stored encrypted,
and the model never sees them.

294
00:15:14,203 --> 00:15:16,963
And a new or changed
script has a fingerprint:

295
00:15:16,963 --> 00:15:21,101
if you have not approved it,
it asks first, even if you said not to ask.

296
00:15:21,451 --> 00:15:25,573
And MCP servers:
outside programs that offer tools,

297
00:15:25,573 --> 00:15:28,394
over standard input or over HTTP.

298
00:15:28,654 --> 00:15:30,666
They can be imported from Claude Desktop.

299
00:15:31,016 --> 00:15:32,845
Every tool has the same parts.

300
00:15:33,105 --> 00:15:34,105
A name in English.

301
00:15:34,365 --> 00:15:35,745
A description for the model.

302
00:15:36,005 --> 00:15:38,212
Its parameters, as a JSON schema.

303
00:15:38,472 --> 00:15:39,437
Its examples.

304
00:15:39,697 --> 00:15:40,388
Its risk.

305
00:15:40,648 --> 00:15:41,872
Whether it only reads.

306
00:15:42,132 --> 00:15:43,528
And whether it can be undone.

307
00:15:45,778 --> 00:15:46,584
Now, the loop.

308
00:15:46,844 --> 00:15:49,904
Everything goes to the model,
and the reply streams in.

309
00:15:50,164 --> 00:15:55,176
Either it is text, and that's it, or it is
tool calls, with their arguments in JSON.

310
00:15:55,526 --> 00:15:57,357
Every call goes through the tool runner.

311
00:15:57,617 --> 00:15:59,830
It checks the tool exists
and is switched on.

312
00:16:00,090 --> 00:16:04,208
It writes down in words what it is about
to do, like: set a ten minute timer.

313
00:16:04,468 --> 00:16:07,794
It looks at its risk, with your preferences,
and asks if it has to.

314
00:16:08,144 --> 00:16:10,362
And there are checks
against making things up.

315
00:16:10,622 --> 00:16:14,165
A web address must come from what you said,
or from what the tools said.

316
00:16:14,425 --> 00:16:18,005
And to run one of your commands,
your order has to really ask for it,

317
00:16:18,005 --> 00:16:19,294
not just sound alike.

318
00:16:19,644 --> 00:16:22,575
The result goes back
to the model as one more message.

319
00:16:22,835 --> 00:16:26,345
It is written into the activity,
and if it can be undone,

320
00:16:26,345 --> 00:16:29,187
how to undo it is kept,
even across restarts.

321
00:16:29,537 --> 00:16:30,617
Then another round.

322
00:16:30,877 --> 00:16:34,784
At most three rounds and three calls;
with a big model, five and eight.

323
00:16:35,044 --> 00:16:39,033
A call that already failed is not repeated,
nor one that already worked.

324
00:16:39,293 --> 00:16:41,873
When the limit is reached,
it is told to answer now.

325
00:16:42,223 --> 00:16:46,406
If it names one of your commands without
running it, or says it will send something

326
00:16:46,406 --> 00:16:49,132
without sending it,
it gets one more round to do it.

327
00:16:49,482 --> 00:16:51,254
At the end, what it says is checked.

328
00:16:51,514 --> 00:16:54,394
If it claims it sent,
saved or opened something,

329
00:16:54,394 --> 00:16:57,807
and no tool of that kind worked,
that sentence is removed.

330
00:16:58,067 --> 00:17:00,582
And when the tools have their own words,

331
00:17:00,582 --> 00:17:03,864
like I'll remind you at 5:15,
those are used, exactly.

332
00:17:04,214 --> 00:17:07,704
If it gets stuck, or says nothing,
it thinks it over, with more time.

333
00:17:07,964 --> 00:17:12,396
And if the answer is too long for a speech
bubble, it is written on a reading sheet,

334
00:17:12,396 --> 00:17:14,217
and only the summary is spoken.

335
00:17:16,467 --> 00:17:19,529
Assistants and K-Desktop work
in both directions.

336
00:17:19,879 --> 00:17:22,468
An assistant can fire one of your events,

337
00:17:22,468 --> 00:17:26,380
or create a new one of any kind:
at a time, when a program opens,

338
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:29,410
when the PC is idle,
when you plug in headphones.

339
00:17:29,670 --> 00:17:32,091
K-Desktop builds it, saves it at once,

340
00:17:32,091 --> 00:17:35,614
and it shows up in Events,
marked as made by an assistant.

341
00:17:35,874 --> 00:17:36,970
Undo removes it.

342
00:17:37,320 --> 00:17:41,355
And it can schedule orders: at seven,
check the weather and send it to me.

343
00:17:41,615 --> 00:17:45,167
That creates an event at a time,
with the action ask an assistant,

344
00:17:45,167 --> 00:17:47,337
and the tools you approved right then.

345
00:17:47,687 --> 00:17:52,065
When the time comes, K-Desktop sends
the order as a scheduled heard message.

346
00:17:52,325 --> 00:17:53,429
Nobody is asked:

347
00:17:53,429 --> 00:17:57,440
what was approved is done, and anything
that would have asked is not done.

348
00:17:57,700 --> 00:18:00,746
If the PC was off at that time,
it does not run late.

349
00:18:01,096 --> 00:18:04,845
The other way round, your commands
and events can use the assistants,

350
00:18:04,845 --> 00:18:06,044
with four actions.

351
00:18:06,304 --> 00:18:07,566
Run one of its actions.

352
00:18:07,826 --> 00:18:08,740
Ask it something.

353
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:10,546
Say a text, with no AI.

354
00:18:10,806 --> 00:18:12,509
Or free its brain before a game.

355
00:18:12,859 --> 00:18:16,949
And if you like, the assistant can act
out the commands you launch yourself,

356
00:18:16,949 --> 00:18:18,695
with a hotkey or a QuickMenu.

357
00:18:18,955 --> 00:18:21,614
That is just decoration:
nothing waits for it.

358
00:18:23,864 --> 00:18:24,862
Back to the answer.

359
00:18:25,122 --> 00:18:29,537
While the AI writes, the text reaches
the chat up to eight times a second,

360
00:18:29,537 --> 00:18:32,242
already cleaned up:
no Markdown, no emojis.

361
00:18:32,592 --> 00:18:34,637
And the voice does not wait
for it to finish.

362
00:18:34,897 --> 00:18:39,700
Every sentence that ends, with its full
stop and a space, is sent off to be spoken.

363
00:18:39,960 --> 00:18:43,894
A very long sentence with no full
stop is cut at its last comma.

364
00:18:44,244 --> 00:18:46,670
Kokoro turns it into speech
on your processor.

365
00:18:46,930 --> 00:18:48,682
First the text becomes phonemes.

366
00:18:48,942 --> 00:18:51,391
It makes the first piece,
up to the first comma,

367
00:18:51,391 --> 00:18:53,749
and starts playing it
while it makes the rest.

368
00:18:54,009 --> 00:18:56,140
It takes about a sixth
of the time it lasts.

369
00:18:56,490 --> 00:18:59,081
On top go the effects
of its voice profile:

370
00:18:59,081 --> 00:19:01,776
the Lander sounds
like mission control radio.

371
00:19:02,036 --> 00:19:05,200
It plays from the side of the screen
where the character is,

372
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:08,795
and the level of its voice moves
the character thirty times a second.

373
00:19:09,145 --> 00:19:13,172
While it speaks, K-Desktop remembers
what it is saying, and until when.

374
00:19:13,432 --> 00:19:18,515
So if its wake word turns up in its own
sentence, Hi, I'm Lander, it does not fire.

375
00:19:18,865 --> 00:19:20,168
How long does it all take?

376
00:19:20,428 --> 00:19:23,801
From the moment you stop talking:
a command, under a second.

377
00:19:24,061 --> 00:19:26,580
A rule, just over a second
to the first sound.

378
00:19:26,840 --> 00:19:30,204
And the AI, about a second and a half,
with its model loaded.

379
00:19:32,454 --> 00:19:35,085
When it stops talking,
a few things are still left.

380
00:19:35,345 --> 00:19:37,268
If it ended with a question,

381
00:19:37,268 --> 00:19:41,284
K-Desktop listens for six seconds
with no wake word: just answer.

382
00:19:41,634 --> 00:19:43,829
If you asked it to write what you say,

383
00:19:43,829 --> 00:19:46,671
dictation starts right now,
once its voice is over.

384
00:19:47,021 --> 00:19:50,334
The order and the answer go
into that conversation's history.

385
00:19:50,594 --> 00:19:53,623
And in the background,
the same model reads the exchange

386
00:19:53,623 --> 00:19:56,358
and remembers what is worth
remembering about you.

387
00:19:56,618 --> 00:19:58,946
You can see it, and undo it,
in the activity.

388
00:19:59,296 --> 00:20:02,444
If an order did nothing,
and the next one corrects it,

389
00:20:02,444 --> 00:20:06,589
say, it's called K-Movies, it learns
a new voice phrase for that command.

390
00:20:06,849 --> 00:20:08,649
Next time, it runs straight away.

391
00:20:08,999 --> 00:20:10,650
The character goes back to rest.

392
00:20:10,910 --> 00:20:14,423
And if the brain is not used
for thirty minutes, it is unloaded,

393
00:20:14,423 --> 00:20:16,530
and the graphics card is free again.

394
00:20:18,780 --> 00:20:19,531
To sum up.

395
00:20:19,791 --> 00:20:22,568
The wake word,
with two opinions and a second check.

396
00:20:22,828 --> 00:20:25,828
The order,
until 800 milliseconds of silence.

397
00:20:26,088 --> 00:20:27,081
The fixed rules.

398
00:20:27,341 --> 00:20:29,482
Your commands, by words and by meaning.

399
00:20:29,742 --> 00:20:30,680
The single letter.

400
00:20:30,940 --> 00:20:31,684
Programs.

401
00:20:31,944 --> 00:20:36,795
And only at the end, the AI, with a few
well chosen tools, permissions and checks.

402
00:20:37,055 --> 00:20:39,558
All the way to the voice,
sentence by sentence.

403
00:20:39,907 --> 00:20:41,209
All of it on your PC.

404
00:20:41,469 --> 00:20:42,559
Thanks for watching.
